{
  "compilerOptions": {
    // ES2025 is the newest ECMAScript edition TypeScript ships a `lib` for, and the whole of
    // it is native on the `engines` floor (Node 22). Nothing here is downlevelled: `target`
    // and `lib` name the same edition on purpose, so a feature that type-checks is a feature
    // the runtime has. The step from ES2022 bought four things the tree now uses —
    // `Array.prototype.toSorted`/`toReversed`/`with` and `findLast` (ES2023), `Map.groupBy`
    // and `Object.groupBy` (ES2024), the `Set` algebra methods and the iterator helpers
    // (ES2025).
    "target": "ES2025",
    "lib": ["ES2025", "DOM"],

    // `nodenext`, not `bundler`: this package ships as ESM to Node and every relative import
    // already carries its `.js`, so `nodenext` is what CHECKS that — it resolves the way Node
    // will at runtime, where `bundler` resolved the way a bundler would and no bundler is
    // involved. It also turns `package.json` `exports` into something the compiler verifies
    // rather than something only `npm publish` finds out about.
    "module": "nodenext",
    "moduleResolution": "nodenext",

    "outDir": "dist",
    "rootDir": "src",
    "strict": true,

    // TypeScript 7 defaults `types` to `[]`; this states it, because it is a real fact about
    // this library rather than a default worth inheriting silently. `src/` reaches for no
    // ambient global — no `process`, no `Buffer`, no `__dirname` — so nothing here should be
    // able to acquire one by having `@types/node` merely present in `node_modules`. The test
    // project, which does use them, adds `"node"` back.
    "types": [],

    // Beyond `strict`. Each of these was measured against the tree before being turned on;
    // the zero-cost ones say the codebase already had the discipline and now cannot lose it.
    //
    //   useUnknownInCatchVariables        0 errors — a catch binding really is unknown
    //   noImplicitReturns                 0 errors — every path of a value-returning function returns
    //   verbatimModuleSyntax              0 errors — type-only imports stay erasable and explicit
    //   noUnusedParameters                0 errors — an ignored parameter says so with a `_`
    //   noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature 0 errors — `x.foo` means a declared `foo`, `x['foo']` a looked-up one
    //   noUncheckedSideEffectImports      0 errors — a bare `import './x.js'` must still resolve
    //   noFallthroughCasesInSwitch        1 error  — Midi.ts's one intentional fallthrough is now
    //                                                written out, so accidental ones are catchable
    //   noImplicitOverride               15 errors — an override must say so, which is what stops a
    //                                                base-class rename from silently orphaning one
    //
    // Deliberately still off, with reasons:
    //   exactOptionalPropertyTypes  15 errors (was 26), all of them still the same shape: option
    //                               bags forwarded field by field (`{ seed: options?.seed }`).
    //                               Turning it on today means adding `| undefined` to ~15
    //                               declarations — which loosens the very types it exists to
    //                               tighten. The count halved on its own as the option plumbing
    //                               was rewritten; the flag goes on when it reaches zero.
    //   erasableSyntaxOnly          45 errors, and all 45 are the same construct: a `constructor(
    //                               readonly role: Role, ...)` parameter property, in 14 files.
    //                               Clearing them means writing out a field declaration and an
    //                               assignment for each — strictly more code, to buy the ability
    //                               to run `src/` through a type-stripper. This package ships
    //                               compiled `dist`, so it would buy nothing and cost 45 sites.
    //   noUnusedLocals              1 error, and it is load-bearing:
    //                               `Msm.computeSemicircleCompression` is unused HERE because it
    //                               is unused in `Msm.java:846` too, and it is retained with that
    //                               note so the two trees stay comparable. A flag that forces its
    //                               deletion is a flag that costs parity evidence.
    //   isolatedDeclarations        385 errors. Every exported signature would need writing out;
    //                               `declaration` already emits them correctly today.
    "useUnknownInCatchVariables": true,
    "noImplicitReturns": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature": true,
    "noUncheckedSideEffectImports": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    // `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` began at 885 errors in `src/` and was cleared directory by
    // directory over the functional-core campaign, against a one-way ratchet that refused to
    // let any directory get worse. What matters is that it was cleared by REMOVING THE INDEXING
    // rather than by silencing it: across the whole of it, zero `!` and zero `as T` were added,
    // and the repo's non-null-assertion count fell from 841 to 705 in the same period.
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,

    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "declaration": true,
    "declarationMap": true,
    "sourceMap": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true
  },
  // `src/fitting/**` is excluded from the emitting project only, and only for
  // `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`: it costs 282 errors there and zero anywhere else in `src/`.
  // The code is still typechecked under every other flag above, by `tsconfig.tests.json`,
  // which includes `src/**/*` and turns that one flag off. Delete this entry — not the flag —
  // when the count reaches zero.
  "include": ["src/**/*"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist", "**/*.test.ts"]
}
